    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d241">

                <pb facs="0247" n="241" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg241"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/19265: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Consul General at Casablanca (Russell)</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-04T19:00:00-05:00">February 4, 1942—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>44. For Murphy from Dunn. Find great interest in subject of your letter of
                    January 9 to me. When you see Hibbard<anchor n="50" xml:id="d241-f1"/> give him
                    as much specific and detailed information on subject as possible and I will
                    endeavor to inform you of our policy. [Dunn.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d241-f1" n="50" xml:id="d241-f1-content">
                <p>Frederick P. Hibbard, en route to his post as Counselor of Legation in
                    Liberia.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
